Day-to-day activities are governed by safe work practices, hot work permit process, and contractor management policies and procedures. Sila promotes a learning and questioning environment and strives for continuous improvement with processes in place for incident investigation, management of change, pre-startup safety review, and compliance auditing. 

Sila encourages team member participation in all aspects of the PSM/RMP Program elements. Team member consultation and participation are sought and achieved through various methods including:
?	PHA and recommendations;
?	incident investigations and recommendations;
?	operating and maintenance procedures development and recertification;
?	management of change (MOC) review and recommendations;
?	training content development;
?	emergency planning and response; and
?	other PSM/RMP program elements and recommendations as needed.

The five-year accident history:

The Sila Moses Lake plant has not had any releases that meet the RMP five-year accident history criteria.

The emergency response program:

Sila developed a written Emergency Response Plan for the Facility to satisfy the emergency planning, response, and notification requirements of the federal, OSHA, and EPA regulations. This plan outlines the responsibilities and actions required to control an emergency specific to the types of incidents that could occur at the Facility. An Emergency Response Team at the Facility is trained to respond to medical and fire emergencies. Sila partnered with the City of Moses Lake Fire Department and the Grant County Local Emergency Planning Committee in case additional support is needed. The City of Moses Lake Fire Department is also a key partner in both tabletop and field exercises to ensure consistent
